<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Always Pack Lunch. – Lesson # 4</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Always Pack Lunch.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Edit classmates' written work.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write instructions for tasks related to a beach trip (e.g., 'Pack a towel and sunscreen.'). Can develop short drafts about beach activities (e.g., 'At the beach, I swim.'). Can revise peers' instructions for clarity (e.g., add 'bring water' to the checklist).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; background-color: #f7f7f7;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; background-color: #f7f7f7;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; background-color: #f7f7f7;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of a beach with people packing different items. Students complete a "Beach Bag Checklist" on the board, writing words for things they would pack. For example, the teacher writes "I always pack a ____" and students write "towel," "sunscreen," "swimsuit." This activates vocabulary related to the scenario and theme in written form.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two simple instructions on the board about a beach trip, demonstrating clear handwriting and sentence structure. For example: "Please pack a sun hat." and "Don't forget your flip-flops." The teacher explicitly models how to form letters and words like 'beach' and 'pack', paying attention to the sounds /b/ and /p/. The teacher also models polite instruction-giving using "Please."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up and modeled instructions.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do I always pack for the beach?" (Students point to a picture of a cooler or respond with 'lunch').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'Please pack a towel' a question or an instruction?" (Expected answer: 'An instruction').</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Trip to the Beach")</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short written notice or a set of simple instructions from a parent to a child about preparing for a beach trip, titled "Your Beach Trip Checklist." It uses imperatives, present simple, and 'going to'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:
          <br>"Hi, Leo! We are going to the beach this Saturday! Please, pack your swimsuit and a towel. Don't forget your sunscreen and a sun hat. I always pack lunch for us. We can build sandcastles and collect shells. What are you going to do at the beach? Have fun! Love, Mom."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing trips ('I always pack lunch for the trip.') and describing routines ('We go to the beach every Saturday.') using written input.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the "Your Beach Trip Checklist" individually.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the message about?" (A trip to the beach.) "Who is going to the beach?" (Leo and Mom/We.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students write T or F next to statements: "Leo needs to pack flip-flops." (F - not mentioned). "Mom always packs lunch." (T).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Draw a line from the item to the action: "Swimsuit" to "pack," "Sandcastles" to "build."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing using Present simple (affirmative and question forms), Imperatives, and 'Going to'. Incorporating vocabulary: beach, towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, sand, shells, pack, bring, forget, swim, relax, build, collect, sunny, sandy, fun. Phonemic awareness: Spelling one-syllable or two-syllable words (e.g., 'beach,' 'sandy,' 'pack').</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Sentence Scramble & Complete."</b> The teacher provides sentences with scrambled words or missing words related to packing and beach activities. Students rewrite the sentences correctly or fill in the blanks.
              <br>Example: "a / pack / towel / Please." → "Please pack a towel."
              <br>Example: "I am _____ to the beach." (going)
              <br>Example: "We often ______ shells." (collect)
              <br>This activity reinforces correct sentence structure and spelling of target vocabulary.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: "My Beach Day Instructions."</b> Students individually write 3-4 simple instructions for a friend going to the beach. The teacher provides sentence frames to guide them, e.g., "Please pack your _______." "Don't forget _______." "I am going to _______." "Do you like to _______?" They should use at least three vocabulary words from the list (e.g., swimsuit, sunscreen, beach ball). Emphasis on writing polite instructions.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Writing & Peer Check: "Compare Your Instructions."</b> In pairs, students exchange their "My Beach Day Instructions." They read their partner's instructions and check for:
              <br>a) Clear instructions (Can they understand what to do?).
              <br>b) Correct spelling of beach-related words (e.g., 'beach,' 'sandy').
              <br>c) Use of 'Please' for politeness.
              <br>They mark any unclear parts or spelling errors with a light pencil dot, without correcting them directly. This prepares them for editing.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br><p style="text="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Trip to the Beach")</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative writing and peer editing of a "Beach Trip Plan." Students will work in pairs to write a short plan for a day at the beach, then exchange with another pair for revision.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Draft a "Beach Trip Plan":</b> In their original pairs, students write a short plan (3-5 sentences) for their imaginary trip to the beach. They must include:
              <br>a) When they are going (e.g., "We are going to the beach on Saturday.").
              <br>b) At least two things they will pack (e.g., "Please pack a cooler and sunscreen.").
              <br>c) At least two activities they will do (e.g., "We are going to build sandcastles and swim in the waves.").
              <br>d) One routine (e.g., "I always pack lunch for our trip.").
              <br>They should focus on using imperatives and 'going to' correctly.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Editing (Addressing Specific Objective):</b> Pairs exchange their written "Beach Trip Plan" with another pair. Each pair acts as an 'editor' for the other group's plan. They use a simple checklist provided by the teacher to check their classmates' written work:
              <br>  - Are all sentences clear? (e.g., can you understand what they mean?)
              <br>  - Did they include what to pack? (Yes/No)
              <br>  - Did they include activities? (Yes/No)
              <br>  - Is 'Please' used when giving instructions? (Yes/No)
              <br>  - Any spelling mistakes (e.g., 'beach,' 'sand')? Circle them.
              <br>  - Suggest one thing to add to make it better (e.g., "Add: bring water.").
              <br>Students write their comments and suggestions on a separate feedback slip or directly on the draft if instructed.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Revision:</b> Pairs receive their original plan back with peer feedback and make quick revisions based on the suggestions, focusing on clarity and correctness.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a simple rubric/checklist focused on the writing and editing process.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will submit their *revised* "Beach Trip Plan" along with the peer feedback slip. The teacher will review both to see how effectively students incorporated feedback and edited their work.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of instructions/sentences in the revised plan (Are the instructions easy to understand?).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., swimsuit, sunscreen, build, collect).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of imperatives and 'going to'.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of revision based on peer feedback (e.g., corrected spelling, added information).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Application of polite language ("Please") in instructions.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing instructions today is..."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I helped my classmate by suggesting to add/change..."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">7-8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Post-lesson reflection)</p></td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the beach plan was easy for you? What was difficult?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did revising your partner's work help you with your own writing?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing-focused lesson? (e.g., Peer editing was effective in highlighting common errors.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with sentence formation or vocabulary recall for the next writing task? (e.g., Provide more sentence starters, offer a word bank during independent writing tasks.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their understanding by describing their beach trip plan to another class or family member, potentially using their revised written plan as a basis for a short presentation or a mediated message. They will integrate all four skills to summarize and present their beach trip information.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>